- char:setBasicInfo() is now updated regularly, but will not do expected things like add racials
  to the ability list because abilities are only added in setSpecAndTalents(), which is not called
  regularly (for good reason).

- EVENT ARCHITECTURE add :consume(), consider moving finalizeInference/trackCooldown to Event
- :consume() - event > ability inferences should somtimes consume the evidence associated with them.
               this essentially assigns evidence to an event.
  there must be a no-side effects version of consume
  PROBLEM: ringbuffer FIFO is a bad data structure for removing arbitrary elements
    - rather than remove, set the item to nil or whatever the initializer element was?
    - solution: ringbuffer becomes 2-tuples of (timestamp, #events), solving 2 problems:
        a. can squish multiple simultaneous events into a single record, reducing the need for
           a large queue size
        b. can set #events=0 when they're consumed and leave the count=0 records in the ringbuffer
  Examples:
    a. must consume: GoAK vs. cheat. there are two relevant evidentiary events: a spellcast and a debuff.
       if GoAK is used at the same time cheat procs, these events are too close to the buff add/updates
       to use them to differentiate which happened first. but, because they're so close together, it doesn't
       really matter which happened first, just make an assignment and move on and tolerate a <0.1s inaccuracy
       in the CD tracker.
       TO DO THIS: either: GoAK *consumes* the spellcast event when it is assigned or cheat *consumes* the
       debuff when it is assigned. consumption means the evidentiary event is no longer present in a followup
       inference.
    b. must not consume: AoE CCs. an AoE CC cast creates one spellcast event but may cause many CCs. the point
       is that consumption is not always correct

