- behavior to solve with updates/batch inferences
    - MUST DO: ensure new batch inference doesn't trigger feedback on every certain inference
        * it seems this is the most natural design. AURA(update) events are always created (even
          for certain inferences - multi-charge abilities must do this), then data sharing through
          a shared aura object implicitly incurs "batch" inference when inferAbility() is called
          in sequence across events in a batch.
    - good test: sentinel - is a self updater that doesn't instant ID because of bubble
    - if an aura update arrives before a certain ID (e.g., in this case because we can't rule out
      bubble until no debuff is observed in the tolerance window), an update event is added to
      sentinel's batch. later, when the first AURA(add) event is IDed with certainty, it is no
      longer the event checked by the AURA(update) handler. instead, the update event is. the
      update event will never ID because the original add event put sentinel on CD.
        * the result is a cascade of update events that trigger because of the second update
          event being uncertain.
        * this now rarely happens because of the tolerance tail re-evaluation, but it is still
          possible and the system should be built to handle this
    - the answer to all of the above was to remove events from the queue when there are 0 possible
      solutions under permissive evidence filters

