- better guessing for:
    - duration_variable (should record whether this is < or > the stated duration)
        - in some cases fully unambiguous solutions remain. e.g.: DK AMS (<=7s) and
          IBF (=8s). if these are the only available spells for the group, then any
          duration <= 7s is definitely AMS and any 8s duration is definitely IBF.
        - in others, it becomes a best match. e.g.: paladin sac (<=12s) and just
          about every defensive. since the sac *CAN* end before 12s if the paladin
          drops below 20%, there's a chance it happens to wear off at exactly the
          duration of another defensive causing a misidentification. since the
          likelihood of this is low, the timer is still almost always useful.
            - because of these cases, it could be useful to keep an internal table
              of last-use of cooldowns. if an unambiguous sac happened (e.g.,
              a DK in a group with just a prot paladin and no other externals had
              a defensive that lasted 12s, then it was a sac) then we now know sac
              is unavailable for 60s, meaning that ambiguities caused by sac can
              be ignored for that 60s window.
